.TH DOCKER-HELPER 1
.SH NAME
docker-helper \- Policy-enforcing Docker mediation daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B docker-helper
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.BI "COMMAND"
[\& ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B docker-helper
is a policy-enforcing daemon that provides a restricted interface to Docker.
It mediates Docker operations for coding agents running on the same machine,
restricting host path access to each session's issued filesystem snapshot
(the workspace plus any issued additional roots) and enforcing policy on
build, pull, and run operations.
.PP
Deployment mode is determined by effective UID:
.IP \(bu 4
\fIUser mode\fR (non-root): Private Unix socket, per-user configuration
.IP \(bu 4
\fISystem mode\fR (root): System Unix socket + loopback HTTP, system-wide configuration
.SH QUICK START
.SS User mode
Single-user deployment. Normal docker-helper user-mode operation runs
non-root, but initial host preparation for a rootful Docker installation
may require root — notably adding the user to the
\fBdocker\fR group (`sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"`, logout and login
required). After that preparation, no root is needed for package
installation and everyday docker-helper use. System-mode Principals do
not need \fBdocker\fR group membership; the root daemon mediates Docker
access for them.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
docker-helper init
systemctl --user enable --now docker-helper
mkdir \-p ~/myproject
docker-helper session create ~/myproject
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export the \fBTOKEN\fR printed by \fBsession create\fR (starts with \fIdht_...\fR):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
export DOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN='dht_...'
docker-helper pull alpine:3.24
docker-helper run alpine:3.24 -- echo hello-from-docker-helper
.fi
.RE
.PP
User mode does not require principals or credentials.
.SS System mode
Multi-user deployment. Requires root for initial setup.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
sudo docker-helper init
sudo systemctl enable --now docker-helper
sudo docker-helper principal create --issue\-credential alice
.fi
.RE
.PP
The credential is issued together with the Principal: the token is shown
once \(em the CLI immediately prints the canonical install hint \(em and
never again.
.PP
On alice's machine (not as root):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
docker-helper credential install
mkdir \-p ~/myproject
docker-helper session create ~/myproject
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export the \fBTOKEN\fR printed by \fBsession create\fR (starts with \fIdht_...\fR):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
export DOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN='dht_...'
docker-helper pull alpine:3.24
docker-helper run alpine:3.24 -- echo hello-from-docker-helper
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBcredential install\fR reads the token from stdin and stores it for
subsequent use. After installation, \fBdocker-helper\fR commands use the
credential and connect to the correct daemon automatically.
.SH DEPLOYMENT MODES
.SS User Mode
Runs as the current user. Configuration at
.IR ~/.config/docker-helper/config.json ,
state at
.IR ~/.local/state/docker-helper .
No mandatory MAC integration.
.SS System Mode
Runs as root. Configuration at
.IR /etc/docker-helper/config.json ,
state at
.IR /var/lib/docker-helper .
Requires exactly one MAC backend: AppArmor with managed AppArmor MAC boundaries, or
enforcing SELinux with the daemon in \fBdocker_helper_t\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS General Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper serve
Start the docker-helper daemon.
.TP
.B docker-helper init [\-\-allowed-root PATH]
Initialize configuration and admin token. The interactive default is the
current user's home, or /home for root. Root may explicitly select /home or
/opt; non-root callers cannot select those broad roots directly.
In system mode, the allowed root is the system-wide authorization ceiling;
init does not prepare MAC state, which is handled by the session lifecycle
at session creation. User mode requires no MAC preparation.
.TP
.B docker-helper reload [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json]
Reload configuration. Narrowing the global allowed-root ceiling atomically
prunes stored Principal roots the new ceiling no longer contains and then
restricted-Launcher descendants outside the surviving per-Principal ceilings
before the new runtime policy is published; existing Session filesystem
snapshots are not rewritten. A reconciliation failure commits no descendant
deletions and leaves the current runtime configuration active. With
\-\-json the structured acknowledgement (\fBreloaded\fR) replaces the human
line.
.TP
.B docker-helper version [\-\-json]
Print version information (\fB{version}\fR under \-\-json).
\fBdocker-helper --version\fR and \fBdocker-helper \-v\fR are aliases.
.TP
.B docker-helper help [COMMAND [SUBCOMMAND ...]]
Show help for any command path, for example
.BR "docker-helper help principal credential rotate" .
.TP
.B docker-helper completion bash
Generate Bash completion.
.TP
.B docker-helper completion roots principal [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-stored] [\-\-authority-only] [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE]
Machine-facing completion query: print the target Principal's effective allowed roots, one path per line. The target is \-\-principal when given, otherwise it is inferred from the authenticated credential. The daemon authorizes the query. With \fB\-\-stored\fR it instead prints the target Principal's stored roots — the universe of the Principal allowed-root existing-entity mutations (remove, set-access). With \fB\-\-authority-only\fR the command instead prints only the authenticated operator authority (admin, principal, or launcher) for shell completion introspection; it changes nothing about authorization.
.TP
.B docker-helper completion roots session [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-launcher LAUNCHER] [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE]
Machine-facing completion query: print the effective allowed roots of the Launcher a Session created right now with this authority would use, one path per line. The typed \fB\-\-principal\fR/\fB\-\-launcher\fR selectors are forwarded to the daemon, which resolves the same target a real session create with those selectors would use.
.TP
.B docker-helper completion roots launcher [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-launcher LAUNCHER] [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE]
Machine-facing completion query: print the stored allowed roots of the target Launcher (the default Launcher of the \fB\-\-principal\fR-named Principal, otherwise the Principal inferred from the credential, or the typed positional LAUNCHER), one path per line — the universe of the launcher allowed-root existing-entity mutations.
.TP
.B docker-helper completion selectors principal [\-\-command PATH] [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE]
Machine-facing completion query: print the Principal names the authenticated authority may target with a \-\-principal selector on the completed command path, one per line. Only an admin authority receives the daemon's Principal list; a Principal credential receives exactly its own username on the Launcher command families and on the \fBprincipal show\fR USER positional, and nothing where the selector is contractually illegal (both Session command paths); every other authority degrades silently.
.TP
.B docker-helper completion selectors launcher [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE]
Machine-facing completion query: print the Launcher selectors the authenticated authority may target with a \-\-launcher selector or the positional [LAUNCHER] argument, one per line, honoring the typed \-\-principal context. An admin with a context receives that Principal's Launcher names; an admin without one receives only globally resolvable \fBdhl_\fR Launcher IDs; a Principal credential receives its own Launchers' names; a Launcher credential and any foreign scope receive nothing.
.SS Session Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper session create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-filesystem-root PATH=ACCESS]... [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-launcher LAUNCHER] [\-\-json] WORKSPACE
Create a new session for a workspace. WORKSPACE is the one required
positional operand: a workspace-relative path or an absolute host path
inside the authenticated authority's effective allowed roots. WORKSPACE
must resolve to a proper descendant of an effective allowed root; the
allowed root itself is an authority ceiling, not a valid Session
workspace (the daemon canonicalizes the workspace and enforces the
strict-inside containment). With \-\-json, output in JSON format.
The repeatable \fB\-\-filesystem-root PATH=ACCESS\fR flag issues additional
filesystem roots for the Session: PATH is an absolute host path inside the
resolved target Launcher's effective allowed roots and must already exist
as a directory or regular file before Session creation — Docker Helper
does not create a missing requested root, and a missing admitted root is
refused with the documented \fBinvalid_filesystem_policy\fR
path-resolution contract — and ACCESS is
exactly \fBread_write\fR or \fBread_only\fR. The workspace itself remains
mandatory and receives the maximum access the effective policy permits at
that path; a root whose canonical path equals the workspace explicitly
narrows it instead. The request may only narrow the effective Launcher
ceiling, never widen it: a \fBread_write\fR root at a path where the
effective Launcher ceiling is read_only — or any path outside the
ceiling — is refused with \fB400 invalid_filesystem_policy\fR before the
Session exists. Within one request's own entries a more-specific
\fBread_write\fR exception below a broader \fBread_only\fR Session region
is legal when the effective Launcher ceiling authorizes \fBread_write\fR
at that child: a Session may be read-only over a broader tree with a
more-specific writable child. Omitting the
flag preserves the inherited create behavior. The CLI validates
PATH=ACCESS syntax only; whether the request is a valid narrowing is decided
by the daemon. The issued snapshot is immutable; there is no post-create
Session filesystem mutation.
In user mode every requested root must be the canonical workspace itself
(user mode has no inode-pinned mounts); any other root is refused with the
same error.
The target Launcher is chosen by the daemon. Without a selector the
authority-specific defaults apply: a Principal credential resolves its
Principal's \fBdefault\fR Launcher; a Launcher credential is forced to its
own Launcher (which does not need to be named \fBdefault\fR); a user-mode
admin token resolves the local daemon-owner default Launcher; a system-mode
admin token requires an explicit selector (\fB400
missing_launcher_selector\fR). With an admin token,
\fB\-\-principal USER\fR targets that Principal's default Launcher and
\fB\-\-launcher LAUNCHER\fR a specific Launcher (a name requires
\fB\-\-principal USER\fR because names are principal-scoped; a Launcher ID
\fIdhl_...\fR is forwarded as-is). With a principal credential,
\fB\-\-launcher\fR accepts a name or ID visible to that credential; the name
is resolved to the Launcher's global ID through the daemon's scope-first
launcher list query, so a foreign or missing Launcher is the non-disclosing
\fBlauncher not found\fR. A launcher credential has no Launcher
control-plane authority: no selector is needed, because the authenticated
Launcher is forced by the credential itself; supplying that Launcher's own
\fIdhl_...\fR ID is accepted as explicit self-selection and does not
change the target (the ID is reported for the credential by
\fBGET /auth\fR). A foreign ID reaches daemon authorization and answers
with the non-disclosing \fBlauncher not found\fR, while a name is rejected
locally with a hint to use the \fIdhl_...\fR ID. \fB\-\-principal\fR is only valid with an admin
token. The two selectors are mutually exclusive on the wire: the resolved
target is sent as either \fBprincipal\fR or \fBlauncher_id\fR, never both.
.TP
.B docker-helper session list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-launcher LAUNCHER] [\-\-json]
List active sessions. With \-\-json, output in JSON format. The optional
selectors can only narrow the authenticated authority's visible sessions,
never expand it: with an admin token \fB\-\-principal USER\fR narrows the
list to that Principal's sessions and \fB\-\-launcher LAUNCHER\fR to one
Launcher's sessions (a name requires \fB\-\-principal USER\fR because
Launcher names are principal-scoped and are never searched globally; a
global Launcher ID \fIdhl_...\fR is sufficient without it). With a
principal credential \fB\-\-launcher\fR accepts a name or ID inside that
credential's own scope and \fB\-\-principal\fR is invalid; with a launcher
credential the list stays restricted to that launcher's sessions and the
selectors are invalid. A missing or foreign target is the non-disclosing
\fBlauncher not found\fR / \fBprincipal not found\fR.
.TP
.B docker-helper session show [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] SESSION_ID [\-\-json]
Show one session: its public metadata plus the persisted immutable
\fBFILESYSTEM SNAPSHOT\fR in its exact canonical ordering (an explicit
PATH/ACCESS table in human output; the access mode is never hidden).
Authorization follows the Session-control matrix (admin token, Principal
credential, or Launcher credential; a session bearer has no introspection
authority), and a missing or foreign session is the non-disclosing
\fB404 session_not_found\fR. The snapshot is the persisted state loaded by
the daemon's canonical snapshot loader, never a recomputation of current
policy.
.TP
.B docker-helper session delete [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] SESSION_ID [\-\-json]
Delete a session by ID. SESSION_ID is the one required positional operand,
matching \fBsession show SESSION_ID\fR. With \-\-json, output in JSON format.
.TP
.B docker-helper self [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json]
Introspect the authenticated credential's own identity. The daemon
classifies the bearer credential and answers with the matching self
resource; the CLI performs no local classification and takes no target.
A Principal credential answers with its Principal (username, uid/gid,
home, enabled state, stored and effective allowed-root entries), a
Launcher credential with its Launcher (id, name, owning principal,
enabled state, scope, stored and effective allowed-root entries), and a
Session bearer with its Session's own \fBsession show\fR body
(workspace, ownership, expiry, persisted immutable filesystem snapshot).
The admin token has no self resource and receives the stable
\fB404 self_not_available\fR; unknown, revoked, disabled, or expired
credentials receive the non-disclosing 401 authentication contract. The
endpoint is read-only. With \-\-json, output the raw response envelope
(\fBok\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBresource\fR): the envelope's \fBtype\fR
discriminates which of the three self resource shapes \fBresource\fR
carries, so the union cannot project one fixed bare resource schema.
.PP
self is the one dual-authority introspection surface, so the credential
source is selected explicitly: \fB\-\-token\-file\fR wins; without it a
non-empty \fBDOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN\fR is the Session bearer; with
neither, the normal operator credential source answers. The daemon still
classifies the selected bearer; the CLI only selects the credential
source. This is not shared with the other command families: agent/data-plane
commands (pull, build, run, registry login) authenticate only with
\fBDOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN\fR and never fall back to an installed
credential, and session control commands (session create/list/show/delete)
authenticate only through the operator credential source, which
\fBDOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN\fR never overrides. To exercise a specific
authority, provide only the intended source or select it explicitly with
\fB\-\-token\-file\fR; do not rely on ambient credentials.
.TP
.B docker-helper session cleanup [\-\-json]
Remove expired sessions from the local state database and clean up stale
session runtime directories. Session runtime directories may contain
session-scoped Docker registry credentials. This is intentionally offline
local-state maintenance: the daemon must be stopped while the command runs,
and the command must run locally on the daemon host/environment whose state
database is being maintained. It has no endpoint/authentication flags
(\fB\-\-system\fR, \fB\-\-endpoint\fR, \fB\-\-token\-file\fR): it operates
directly on the local SQLite database and runtime directory, not through
the API, and requires no token. With \-\-json the structured
result (\fBremoved\fR) replaces the human count line.
.SS Config Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper config show [\-\-json] [FIELD]
Show configuration values. Without FIELD, prints complete effective config as JSON.
\fBallowed_roots\fR is the authoritative rich projection of the
stored global allowed roots.
.TP
.B docker-helper config set [\-\-json] FIELD VALUE
Set a configuration value. Writable fields: session_ttl, log_level,
audit_enabled, shutdown_timeout, operation_retention_ttl, operation_max_completed,
operation_log_max_bytes, trusted_ca_path, trusted_ca_injection, http_address.
Workspace roots are managed through \fBconfig allowed-root\fR.
.TP
.B docker-helper config unset [\-\-json] FIELD
Unset a configuration value. Unsettable fields: log_level, audit_enabled,
shutdown_timeout, operation_retention_ttl, operation_max_completed,
operation_log_max_bytes, trusted_ca_path, trusted_ca_injection, http_address.
.TP
.B docker-helper config allowed-root list [\-\-json]
List global allowed roots, one canonical root per line (authorization
ceiling); \-\-json prints the canonical rich entries with access modes.
The list is the recovery-safe stored inspection: a stale entry whose
directory was deleted outside docker-helper stays visible so the operator
can remove it, while daemon startup and reload keep failing closed on it.
.TP
.B docker-helper config allowed-root add [\-\-json] [--access ACCESS] PATH
Add a global allowed root. Changes the authorization ceiling only;
never prepares MAC state. In system mode, MAC coverage for a concrete
workspace is handled by the session lifecycle at session creation.
Omitted \fB\-\-access\fR is the canonical read_write grant. With
\-\-json the shared structured add result (\fBpath\fR, \fBaccess\fR,
\fBchanged\fR) replaces the human acknowledgement.
.TP
.B docker-helper config allowed-root set-access [\-\-json] PATH read_only|read_write
Change the access mode (read_write or read_only) of one stored global
allowed root, matched by the stored canonical identity. A root that is
not stored is an error, not an idempotent no-op; an unchanged access is
reported as success. On success the command prints
\fBchanged PATH to access MODE\fR or \fBunchanged PATH (access MODE)\fR;
a write that also carried the legacy-schema migration reports that fact
in the line. With \-\-json the shared structured set-access result
(\fBpath\fR, \fBaccess\fR, \fBchanged\fR) is printed instead, and
\fBmigrated\fR is true exactly when the write carried the legacy-schema
migration.
.TP
.B docker-helper config allowed-root remove [\-\-json] PATH
Remove a global allowed root. Narrowing the global ceiling cascades the
stored principal and launcher roots the new ceiling no longer contains
before the narrowed policy is published (runtime reload; a config change
made while the daemon is stopped reconciles at startup). With \-\-json
the shared structured remove result (\fBpath\fR, \fBchanged\fR) replaces
the human acknowledgement.
.SS Principal Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper principal create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-issue-credential | \-\-no-credential] [\-\-json] USER
Create a new principal. By default the CLI prompts whether to issue an
initial principal credential; the prompt is suppressed by
\fB\-\-issue-credential\fR (issue) or \fB\-\-no-credential\fR (do not).
In non-interactive use one of these flags is required. The default output
is the human principal block (the one-time initial-credential token shown
once, install hint on stdout); \-\-json prints the canonical creation
document with the install hint on stderr.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json]
List all principals (username, UID, GID, home, enabled).
.TP
.B docker-helper principal show [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER [FIELD]
Show principal details. The default output is the compact human identity
block with the stored allowed-roots PATH/ACCESS table; \fB\-\-json\fR
prints the canonical JSON document, and the optional FIELD positional
extracts one scalar value (exclusive of \fB\-\-json\fR). Read authority is
scope-first: an admin token
reads any Principal, a principal credential reads exactly the Principal
it authenticated as (the daemon authorizes the target; the CLI performs
no local self-check), and a foreign selector is the same non-disclosing
not-found as a missing Principal.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal set [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER FIELD VALUE
Modify principal settings. FIELD currently supports only \fBenabled\fR;
VALUE must be \fBtrue\fR or \fBfalse\fR.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal delete [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER
Delete a principal and all its launchers, sessions, credentials, and
allowed roots. The delete is a checked teardown: it fails with
\fB409 launcher_runtime_active\fR while any launcher still has active
runtime, then removes the principal (launchers via FK cascade).
.TP
.B docker-helper principal allowed-root add [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-access ACCESS] [\-\-json] USER PATH
Add an allowed root for a principal. Omitted \fB\-\-access\fR is the
canonical read_write grant.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal allowed-root set-access [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER PATH read_only|read_write
Change the access mode (read_write or read_only) of one stored principal
allowed root, matched by the stored canonical identity. The success
presentation is the one shared set-access owner:
\fBchanged PATH to access MODE on principal USER\fR or
\fBunchanged PATH (access MODE) on principal USER\fR; with \-\-json the
shared structured result object (\fBpath\fR, \fBaccess\fR,
\fBchanged\fR) is printed instead.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal allowed-root remove [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER PATH
Remove an allowed root from a principal. The removal is one atomic
policy transition: stored roots of that principal's restricted launchers
that the resulting effective principal ceiling no longer contains are
deleted in the same transaction (a restricted launcher whose last root is
cascaded away stays restricted with zero roots). Removing an absent root
is the unchanged no-op. Already-issued sessions are not invalidated and
their filesystem snapshots are not rewritten.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal allowed-root list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] USER
List a principal's stored allowed roots, one canonical root per line;
\-\-json prints the canonical rich entries with access modes. The read
follows the principal show contract: an admin token lists any Principal,
a principal credential lists exactly its own.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-name NAME] [\-\-json] USER
Create a new credential for a principal. If \-\-name is not specified, defaults to "default". The one-time token appears only in this output; with \-\-json the canonical issuance document (\fBok\fR, \fBcredential\fR, \fBtoken\fR) replaces the human block and the install hint moves to stderr.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] [USER]
List principal credentials as a table with the columns
ID, NAME, CREATED, REVOKED, and PRINCIPAL; with \-\-json, output the
daemon's canonical \fB{ok, credentials}\fR list document instead.
The ID column contains each credential's
\fBdhcr_\fR identifier, which is the value used by \fBcredential revoke\fR.
This is a scope-first list Query: the authenticated authority establishes
the visible credentials (an admin token sees every principal's, a principal
credential sees its own principal's), and the optional USER argument is
only a filter that can narrow that visibility, never expand it. A foreign
or unknown USER is the same non-disclosing not-found error as any other
principal endpoint; no auth introspection happens in the CLI.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential revoke [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] CREDENTIAL_ID
Revoke a credential by its credential ID. With \-\-json the daemon's
revocation document replaces the human "revoked" line.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential rotate [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-name NAME] [\-\-json] [USER]
Rotate a principal credential through the shared database-backed
response-delivery transaction. The credential ID, name, and ownership are
unchanged and the replacement token is printed once on stdout; the
default output is the human credential block with the one-time token
(install hint on stdout), and \-\-json prints the canonical issuance
document with the install hint on stderr.
Before the database commit, the daemon fully serializes the response,
CAS-checks the exact target credential and its current bearer hash, and
writes that response. A response write failure rolls back the rotation,
so the previous bearer remains valid. A concurrent rotation that changes
the bearer first fails with
\fB409 credential_rotation_conflict\fR and returns no replacement token.
After a successful response write the daemon attempts the commit exactly
once. If that commit reports an error, the result is deliberately
ambiguous because the replacement bearer may already have reached the
caller; the command does not retry automatically and operator
re-issue/recovery is required. On the normal committed path the
replacement is the one active bearer and the previous bearer is invalid.
The credential name defaults to "default"; \-\-name selects another
named credential. The principal defaults to the owner of the authenticated
principal credential; with an admin token USER is required.
.SS Launcher Commands
Like the other operator command families, every launcher command accepts
the common operator flags
.BR \-\-system ,
.B \-\-endpoint\ ENDPOINT ,
and
.BR \-\-token-file\ FILE ;
they appear in each synopsis below.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-allowed-root PATH]... [\-\-issue-credential | \-\-no-credential] [\-\-json] NAME
Create a launcher with the name NAME (the one required positional
operand) for a principal. If \fB\-\-principal\fR is omitted it is
inferred from the authenticated credential via \fBGET /auth\fR.
\fBlauncher create default\fR is an ordinary explicit attempt to create the
\fBdefault\fR Launcher; the daemon's canonical conflict path decides it.
A principal's \fBdefault\fR launcher is provisioned automatically at
principal creation, so the explicit attempt conflicts if the principal
already has a launcher named \fBdefault\fR with
\fBlauncher "<name>" already exists for principal "<user>"\fR.
That launcher is a normal, mutable launcher and can later be renamed or
deleted; only the reserved user-mode daemon-owner default Launcher is
permanently protected. Launcher names are path-safe identifiers:
1..63 characters of lowercase letters, digits, and internal hyphens, with
alphanumeric first and last characters; the exact supplied value is
accepted or rejected. With no \fB\-\-allowed-root\fR the scope is
\fBinherit\fR; with one or more it is \fBrestricted\fR. The CLI prompts whether
to issue a launcher credential unless \fB\-\-issue-credential\fR or
\fB\-\-no-credential\fR is given; in non-interactive use one of these flags is
required. The default output is the human launcher block (the one-time
credential token shown once, install hint on stdout); \-\-json prints
the canonical creation document with the install hint on stderr.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-launcher LAUNCHER] [\-\-json]
List launchers with the columns ID, NAME, SCOPE, ENABLED, and PRINCIPAL.
This is a scope-first list Query: the authenticated authority establishes
the visible launchers (an admin token without \fB\-\-principal\fR sees every
principal's launchers; a principal credential sees its own), and the
optional \fB\-\-principal\fR and \fB\-\-launcher\fR flags are only filters
that can narrow that visibility, never expand it. Narrowing happens
server-side: under a resolved Principal scope the Launcher selector accepts
a launcher name or ID; an unfiltered admin must use a launcher ID because
launcher names are principal-scoped and are never searched globally
(\fB\-\-launcher\fR with a name and no \fB\-\-principal\fR is rejected with
\fBlauncher_name_requires_principal\fR). A missing launcher match is a
non-disclosing \fB404 launcher_not_found\fR. No principal inference happens
in the CLI.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher show [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Show launcher details. The default output is the compact human identity
block with the stored allowed-roots PATH/ACCESS table; \fB\-\-json\fR
prints the canonical JSON document.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher set [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-name NAME] [\-\-enabled true|false] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Modify a launcher with at least one of \fB\-\-name\fR or \fB\-\-enabled\fR.
The default output is the human launcher block; \-\-json prints the
canonical launcher document.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher delete [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Delete a launcher. With \-\-json the structured delete result
(\fBlauncher\fR, \fBdeleted\fR) replaces the human line.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher allowed-root add [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-access ACCESS] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER] PATH
Add one allowed root to a launcher. The target-first operand order is
[LAUNCHER] PATH: with no LAUNCHER the Principal's default launcher is
targeted; with one, LAUNCHER is a launcher name or ID (\fBdhl_\fR...) and
PATH the added root. An add on an inherit launcher narrows it
to restricted scope atomically with the insert. Omitted \fB\-\-access\fR
is the canonical read_write grant. With \-\-json the daemon's canonical
narrow mutation document replaces the human acknowledgement.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher allowed-root set-access [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER] PATH read_only|read_write
Change the access mode (read_write or read_only) of one stored launcher
allowed root, matched by the stored canonical identity. The scope mode
is never changed. The success presentation is the one shared set-access
owner: \fBchanged PATH to access MODE on launcher SELECTOR\fR or
\fBunchanged PATH (access MODE) on launcher SELECTOR\fR; with \-\-json
the shared structured result object (\fBpath\fR, \fBaccess\fR,
\fBchanged\fR) is printed instead.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher allowed-root list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
List a launcher's stored allowed roots, one canonical root per line;
\-\-json prints the canonical rich entries with access modes.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher allowed-root remove [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER] PATH
Remove one allowed root from a launcher. Removing the last root leaves
the launcher restricted with an empty root set (fail-closed); only an
explicit inherit returns the launcher to the Principal ceiling. With
\-\-json the daemon's canonical narrow mutation document replaces the
human acknowledgement.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher allowed-root inherit [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Return the launcher to inherit scope (the Principal's effective ceiling)
by the same atomic scope replacement the daemon always used. The default
output is the human launcher block; \-\-json prints the canonical
launcher document.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher credential create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Create a launcher credential. Because a launcher holds at most one
credential, a second create returns the normal already-exists conflict
error. The default output is the human credential block with the bearer
secret shown once (install hint on stdout); \-\-json prints the
canonical issuance document with the install hint on stderr.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher credential show [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Show a launcher's credential metadata. No secret is printed. The default
output is the human credential block; \-\-json prints the canonical
credential document.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher credential rotate [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Rotate a launcher credential through the same database-backed
response-delivery transaction used for principal credentials and print
the replacement bearer once. The credential row keeps its ID, ownership,
Launcher policy, and Sessions; only the bearer secret changes.
The daemon fully serializes the success response before the transaction,
CAS-checks the exact Launcher/credential identity and expected bearer
hash, tentatively replaces the hash, writes the response, and commits only
after the write succeeds. A write failure rolls back and leaves the
previous bearer valid. A competing rotation loses with
\fB409 credential_rotation_conflict\fR and receives no replacement
bearer. A commit error after a successful response write is an ambiguous
outcome: there is no automatic retry and operator re-issue/recovery is
required. On the normal committed path exactly one bearer is active and
the previous bearer is invalid.
The command never rewrites a credential store: the caller is responsible
for installing a returned bearer through the supported credential-install
mechanism. With Launcher authentication, omission selects the
authenticated Launcher. An explicit selector may be that Launcher's own
name or stable \fBdhl_\fR... ID (the exact-own request is constructed
from the authenticated \fBGET /auth\fR projection; the daemon remains
the authorization authority). Any other selector is refused
non-disclosing (\fBlauncher not found\fR) and grants no name-resolution
authority; \fB\-\-principal\fR cannot widen scope. This is the one
credential-management capability of a Launcher credential — it grants no
other Launcher/Principal control-plane authority. Admin-token rotation is
a separate file-backed mechanism and is unchanged.
.TP
.B docker-helper launcher credential delete [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-principal USER] [\-\-json] [LAUNCHER]
Delete a launcher's credential. No secret is printed.
.PP
Except for the Launcher-authenticated credential self-rotation form
described above, LAUNCHER is a launcher name or ID (\fBdhl_\fR + 32 hex
characters), resolved under the principal selected by
\fB\-\-principal\fR or inferred from the authenticated principal
credential; omitting it selects that principal's \fBdefault\fR launcher.
With an admin token \fB\-\-principal\fR is required; the single exception
is an admin targeting an individual launcher by its globally-unique
\fBdhl_\fR ID, where the owning principal is resolved from the daemon's
scope-first launcher list query instead of being named. The CLI never
searches launchers globally by name. Launcher-authenticated self-rotation
instead accepts omission, the authenticated Launcher's own name, or its
stable \fBdhl_\fR ID exactly as documented in the rotate entry above.
.SS Credential Commands
The canonical credential lifecycle commands live under
.BR "principal credential" ;
the top-level
.BR "credential create" ,
.BR "credential list" ,
and
.B credential revoke
spellings are Release 2.0 compatibility aliases that share the same
implementations. The top-level
.B credential install
is canonical.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential create [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-name NAME] [\-\-json] USER
Canonical form of \fBcredential create\fR. Create a new credential for a
principal. If \-\-name is not specified, defaults to "default"; \-\-json
prints the canonical issuance document (\fBok\fR, \fBcredential\fR,
\fBtoken\fR) with the install hint on stderr.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential list [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json] [USER]
Canonical form of \fBcredential list\fR. Run the scope-first principal
credential list Query; USER is an optional narrowing filter; \-\-json
prints the daemon's canonical \fB{ok, credentials}\fR list document.
.TP
.B docker-helper principal credential rotate [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-name NAME] [USER]
Canonical atomic credential rotation (see Principal Commands).
.TP
.B docker-helper credential revoke [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] CREDENTIAL_ID
Revoke a credential by its credential ID.
CREDENTIAL_ID is the credential's unique identifier, prefixed with
\fBdhcr_\fR, printed by \fBprincipal credential create\fR and listed by:
.RS 4
.nf
docker-helper principal credential list USER
.fi
.RE
Revoking permanently invalidates the credential token. The credential
record remains in the database as history, and its name becomes available
for reuse by a new credential.
.TP
.B docker-helper credential install [\-\-json] [\-\-force]
Install a non-admin credential token for system-mode authentication.
The credential may belong to a principal or a launcher; the daemon
resolves its owner and authorization scope when the token is used.
Reads the token from stdin (hidden input on terminal) and stores it
at ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/docker-helper/credential.token
with mode 0600. Must not be run as root.
With \-\-force, replaces an existing credential atomically. With
\-\-json the structured install result (\fBpath\fR) replaces the human
line.
.SS Admin Token Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper admin-token rotate [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-token-file FILE] [\-\-json]
Rotate the admin token. Requires the current admin token
(\fB\-\-token-file\fR is the client auth source, not the destination).
The new token is shown once on stdout; the old token is invalid
immediately and no restart is required. \fB\-\-json\fR prints the API
response.
.SS AppArmor Commands (AppArmor System Mode)
.TP
.B docker-helper apparmor root list [\-\-json]
List managed AppArmor MAC boundaries in the boundary state file, one
name per line (\-\-json prints the bare name array). This is
read-only backend diagnostic inspection: it shows the confinement
resources the Session MAC lifecycle prepared and owns; it is not a
mutation surface and not an authorization API. The Session MAC lifecycle
is the only production writer of managed AppArmor MAC boundaries.
.TP
.B docker-helper apparmor check [\-\-json]
Validate the AppArmor profile. With \-\-json the structured check
result (\fBvalid\fR) replaces the human status line.
.SS SELinux Commands (SELinux System Mode)
.TP
.B docker-helper selinux check [\-\-json]
Validate the installed SELinux system-policy foundation: requires the
docker-helper policy module \fBdocker_helper\fR to be loaded and verifies
docker-helper-owned file contexts against the active policy defaults (the
installed executable is required; optional config/state/runtime paths are
checked only when present). The command is read-only and never mutates
SELinux state. It requires root and uses the existing host MAC authority.
With \-\-json the structured check result (\fBvalid\fR) replaces the
human status line.
.PP
Neither backend exposes a public MAC mutation command. The Session MAC
lifecycle is the only production writer of managed AppArmor MAC
boundaries and of the SELinux fcontext coverage: it prepares the
confinement resources for the concrete filesystem trees issued in the
immutable Session filesystem snapshot at session creation and releases
them through the same lifecycle (the workspace is one such
tree; in system mode additional issued roots participate in the same
lifecycle), and authorization roots are not a second persistent SELinux
root-management API. \fBselinux check\fR remains a read-only diagnostic.
.SS Agent Commands
.TP
.B docker-helper pull [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] IMAGE
Pull a Docker image.
.TP
.B docker-helper build [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] \-\-dockerfile FILE \-\-image NAME [\-\-build-arg KEY=VALUE ...] CONTEXT
Build a Docker image. build requires the CONTEXT positional operand,
\fB\-\-dockerfile FILE\fR, and \fB\-\-image NAME\fR: CONTEXT is a
workspace-relative path or an absolute host path inside the session
workspace (the daemon validates containment), and the Dockerfile path is
relative to the context. \-\-build-arg may be
repeated to pass multiple build arguments.
Build arguments are not a secret transport: a value reaches the
daemon-side Docker CLI child as \-\-build-arg K=V argv and is observable
through /proc/<pid>/cmdline while that child runs (where host procfs
policy permits), and Docker/BuildKit may retain ARG-related material in
image history/provenance.
Run and build are asynchronous operations under fixed concurrency
ceilings (4 per session, 8 per daemon, of which 2 may be builds; a mount
ceiling of 16 caller mounts per run applies): beyond a ceiling the
request is refused immediately with \fBcapacity_unavailable\fR
(HTTP 429, "too many concurrent requests"); there is no queue, and the
client decides whether to retry. A refused request leaves no pin, no
staging, no workload-MAC state and no started container behind. The
same ceilings also bound the synchronous pull and registry-login
executions, which reserve the same shared capacity before their Docker
process starts and never register an operation.
.TP
.B docker-helper run [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] [\-\-entrypoint CMD] [\-\-workdir PATH] [\-\-shm-size SIZE] [\-\-env KEY=VALUE ...] [\-\-env-from DEST=SOURCE ...] [\-\-mount SOURCE:TARGET[:ro] ...] [\-\-helper\-socket] IMAGE [\-\-] [command...]
 Run a Docker container. IMAGE is the one required positional operand: all
docker-helper flags occur before IMAGE, and everything after it belongs
to the workload command; a single optional bare \fB\-\-\fR separator after
IMAGE may be used for clarity. \-\-env, \-\-env-from and \-\-mount may be
repeated.
A run request carries at most 16 caller mounts (duplicates and read-only
mounts included; the server-owned helper-socket projection is not a
caller mount): a longer list is refused with
.BR too_many_mounts .
A mount source is a workspace-relative path (system mode: any file or
subdirectory; user mode: only the workspace root \fB.\fR) or an absolute
host path inside the issued Session filesystem snapshot; any other source
is refused. Mount containment is enforced on the canonical source, not
the caller spelling: the raw source is admitted lexically against the
issued authority before any probing, then canonicalized, and the
canonical source must stay inside the issued Session filesystem snapshot
(the enforcement implementation's intermediate-path/ancestor containment
invariants apply on top; a spelling that only reaches a compliant final
target by violating that containment is not a bypass). A requested writable mount whose source resolves to a read-only region of
the issued Session filesystem snapshot is refused before any container
starts with the \fBread_only_root\fR error \- a policy refusal, distinct
from the structural \fBinvalid_mount\fR. When a source is only needed for
reading, request it read-only (\fB:ro\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-env\-from DEST=SOURCE
Pass an environment variable without placing its value in the
.B docker-helper
command line.
SOURCE names an environment variable of the
.B docker-helper
process itself; its value is read locally and delivered to the container as
DEST through the same environment contract as
.BR \-\-env .
Resolution is fail-closed: an unset SOURCE stops the command before any
container operation is created. A SOURCE that is set but empty is delivered
as an empty value. The value is not placed in the
.B docker-helper
argv, is not printed in diagnostics, is not inherited from the surrounding
environment, and the daemon does not log environment values. Known
limitation (accepted Release 2.2 residual): the run implementation starts
the workload through the legacy Docker CLI, which receives the value as
\-\-env DEST=value, so the value can appear in the argv of that
daemon-side child process for the child's whole execution and is
observable through /proc/<pid>/cmdline where host procfs policy permits.
No alternative secret transport is introduced in Release 2.2; the argv
class closes with the future migration away from the legacy Docker CLI.
When both \-\-env and
\-\-env\-from define the same name, the
\-\-env\-from value wins.
.TP
.B \-\-helper\-socket
Make the helper Unix socket reachable inside the container at
\fI/run/docker-helper/docker-helper.sock\fR (system mode). This is a
server-owned capability: the daemon injects a read-only bind of its own
runtime directory, and the client selects only the boolean flag. While the
projection is active the daemon also supplies the workload with the
server-owned transport locator
\fBDOCKER_HELPER_SOCKET_PATH=/run/docker-helper/docker-helper.sock\fR
when the caller omitted it; an exactly matching caller value is accepted
and a conflicting value is refused as \fBinvalid_helper_socket\fR. The
locator is a path, not a credential. The socket provides transport only;
protected operations still require a bearer credential, passed separately
(for example with
.BR \-\-env\-from ).
Not supported in user mode; the operation is rejected there.
.TP
.B docker-helper registry login [\-\-system] [\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT] \-\-username USER [\-\-password-stdin] [\-\-json] REGISTRY
Log in to a container registry for the current session. REGISTRY is the
one required positional operand.
With \-\-password-stdin, read the password from stdin.
With \-\-json, output in JSON format.
.PP
Agent-facing commands (pull, build, run, registry login) select the daemon
endpoint with \fB\-\-system\fR or \fB\-\-endpoint ENDPOINT\fR and authenticate
with the Session token from \fBDOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN\fR (never a
Principal credential; agent commands do not take \fB\-\-token-file\fR).
Resolution precedence:
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-endpoint\fR (explicit)
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-system\fR (system socket)
.IP \(bu 4
\fBDOCKER_HELPER_SOCKET_PATH\fR
.IP \(bu 4
the existing user-mode socket \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/docker-helper/docker-helper.sock\fR
.IP \(bu 4
the system socket \fB/run/docker-helper/docker-helper.sock\fR
.PP
The presence of \fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR alone does not select a user socket;
agent commands fall back to the system socket when no user-mode daemon is
present. \fB\-\-system\fR and \fB\-\-endpoint\fR are mutually exclusive.
.SH OPERATOR ENDPOINT SELECTION
For launcher, reload, session, principal, credential, completion, and
admin-token rotate commands, select the target daemon:
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-system\fR: Connect to system daemon (default for system mode)
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-endpoint URL\fR: Explicit endpoint: \fI/absolute/path\fR, \fIunix:///absolute/path\fR, or \fIhttp://127.0.0.1:PORT\fR (HTTP is loopback only; requires \fB\-\-token-file\fR; Unix sockets auto-resolve token)
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\-\-token-file FILE\fR: Read authentication token from file (optional for Unix sockets)
.PP
\fB\-\-system\fR and \fB\-\-endpoint\fR are mutually exclusive.
\fB\-\-system\fR may use \fB\-\-token\-file\fR for authentication.
Session control commands authenticate only through this operator
credential source; \fBDOCKER_HELPER_SESSION_TOKEN\fR never overrides or
participates in it.
Without explicit endpoint selection, a deterministic default is used:
an existing user socket is selected first; otherwise the system socket is
selected. A non-root caller without \fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR has no resolvable
user socket and selects the system socket. The token source follows that
selection: user admin token for the
user socket, or the installed credential (a Principal or Launcher
credential) for the non-root system-daemon caller, or the root admin token
for the system socket. A failed request is not retried against another
daemon.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Generic operational or runtime failure
.TP
.B 2
Invalid CLI arguments or input (where applicable)
.PP
\fBdocker-helper run\fR returns the container exit code when the container
exits with a non-zero status.
.PP
SIGINT for async build/run operations returns 130.
SIGTERM for async build/run operations returns 143.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/docker-helper/config.json
System mode configuration
.TP
.B /etc/docker-helper/admin.token
System mode admin token
.TP
.B /var/lib/docker-helper/docker-helper.db
System mode state database
.TP
.B /run/docker-helper/docker-helper.sock
System mode Unix socket
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/docker-helper/config.json
User mode configuration
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/docker-helper
User mode state directory
.TP
.B dirname(config_path)/admin.token
Admin token (same directory as config)
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 4
.B docker-helper
mediates Docker access; rootful Docker access is root-equivalent host capability
.IP \(bu 4
Principal credentials, Launcher credentials, and session tokens are
capabilities—protect them accordingly. They are stored as SHA-256 hashes
resolved through database lookup; the admin token uses SHA-256 hashing
with constant-time comparison in memory.
.IP \(bu 4
Every workload runs under the fixed server-owned runtime privilege
floor: the run argv owner emits \fB\-\-cap\-drop ALL\fR and
\fB\-\-security\-opt no\-new\-privileges:true\fR before any backend
option, and no request field can disable or weaken them. The workload
runs as the server-owned \fIUID:GID\fR (the owning Principal's or the
daemon owner's), which is authoritative and never substituted by the
image \fBUSER\fR. The UID:GID identity is not the only runtime
protection: Linux no-new-privileges and the dropped capability set keep
an image-delivered or build-staging-delivered SUID/SGID executable at
the workload's own execution identity, and build-context staging strips
the SUID/SGID privilege bits from every staged file.
.IP \(bu 4
System mode requires exactly one of AppArmor or enforcing SELinux. AppArmor
uses the \fBdocker-helper-system\fR profile. SELinux requires the daemon in
\fBdocker_helper_t\fR and runs containers as
\fBdocker_helper_container_t\fR with MCS confinement.
.IP \(bu 4
Filesystem policy is pathname-based: canonical resolution handles
symlinks, but two authorized pathnames can still name the same inode
through a hard link. A write through a read-write alias is also observed
through a read-only alias; docker-helper does not promise inode-level
immutability or confidentiality between hard-link aliases.
.IP \(bu 4
An allowed root and a Session's issued filesystem snapshot are a
helper-mediated filesystem capability, not a path ceiling over the
Principal's Unix DAC: in system mode the root-owned daemon reads inside
the granted capability regardless of the Principal's own Unix DAC
reach, and a file inside the capability may enter a staged build
context even when the Principal could not read it. \fBread_only\fR
denies the workload a writable host-path exposure; it is not a
confidentiality boundary against the daemon. Workload file access
remains additionally bounded by kernel DAC under the container
credentials (Principal UID:GID, privilege floor), with POSIX ACLs
evaluated against those actual credentials; host supplementary groups
are not propagated, so permissions depending on those group memberships
may differ.
.IP \(bu 4
The daemon's own listeners are local only: a Unix socket, plus a loopback
HTTP listener in system mode. No remote or non-loopback listener exists in
Release 2. This is a statement about the daemon's own transport only: it
does not promise anything about Docker workload or build networking.
Builds and containers use the Docker/BuildKit default network position;
docker-helper provides no workload or build network isolation.
